# CHOWN(2)

## NAME

chown - change owner and group of a file

## SYNOPSIS

```c
int chown(const char *name, int owner, int group);
```

## DESCRIPTION

The file whose name is given by the null-terminated string pointed to by *name* has its *owner*
and *group* changed as specified. Only the super-user may execute this call, because if users
were able to give files away, they could defeat the (nonexistent) file-space accounting
procedures.

## SEE ALSO

chown(1), passwd(5)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Zero is returned if the owner is changed; -1 is returned on illegal owner changes.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_chown** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
